{
  "$comment": "A fleet is ten jobs with ten models. `roleModels.<lane>.chain` is the ordered list of models that can serve that lane, best first, and the first rung the profile's `maxRetention` ceiling allows is the one that runs it. A chain is therefore ordered WITHIN this profile's ceiling, not globally: a ceiling of zero-retention puts the best zero-retention model for the job first, which is not always the best model for the job overall. The router walks the same chain when a route is spent. No model name in a lane is a promise that it will still exist next month, which is why every lane has a chain.",
  "id": "fleet-safe",
  "title": "Ten jobs, zero-retention models only",
  "provider": "opencode",
  "model": "minimax-m3.1-flash-free",
  "family": null,
  "verified": "2026-10-19",
  "retention": "zero-retention",
  "maxRetention": "zero-retention",
  "notes": "The default. Every lane resolves to a model whose provider states a zero-retention policy: no training, nothing kept. The cost is that six of the ten jobs run on a second-choice model - the planner loses beam (30-day retention) and the reviewer loses Nemotron 3 Ultra - so this is the floor, not the ceiling. The chains are ordered within the ceiling rather than copied from `fleet-all`: `locate` takes deepseek because it publishes a `low` effort level that longcat does not, and `vision` takes minimax because it reads an image in 7 seconds. `fleet-all` gives every lane the model built for it.",
  "limits": {
    "context": 1000000,
    "output": 512000
  },
  "supports": {
    "reasoning": true,
    "temperature": false,
    "toolCall": true,
    "structuredOutput": false,
    "attachment": true
  },
  "effort": {
    "levels": [
      "low",
      "medium",
      "high",
      "xhigh",
      "max"
    ],
    "default": "max",
    "reserveShare": 0.12
  },
  "roles": {
